# eviVLP conjugation site registry
#
# STATUS: DATA ONLY. Nothing reads this file yet. The GUI still resolves coat
# proteins in code - chem_GUI.cpp:489 (the VLP dropdown), chem_GUI.cpp
# MonomerStems(), FileProcessor::GetTmvCoatProtein(), and the
# qb_cp{1,2,3}_{{AMINO_ACID}}.pdb substitution in tcl/fix_{a,b,c}_template.pgn.
# Those five places have to agree, and adding a capsid or a site means editing
# and recompiling all of them.
#
# WHY A REGISTRY IS NEEDED, not just tidier: the Qbeta coat protein filename is
# built by substituting the amino-acid abbreviation, so there can only ever be
# ONE lysine file per capsid (qb_cp1_lys.pdb). Three coat-protein versions per
# amino acid - native, mutated, alternative native - cannot be expressed at all
# in the current scheme. That is the blocker, not the inelegance.
#
# FORMAT - two record types, whitespace-separated, # for comments.
#
#   CAPSID <name> <subunits> <assembly_script> <monomer_stems(,)>
#   SITE   <capsid> <aa> <id> <coat_protein(,)> <residues(,)> <kind> <source>
#
#   kind    native = the residue is that amino acid in the wild-type structure
#           mutant = the wild-type residue was changed to get this site
#   id      what a site-selection dropdown would show
#
# EVERY ROW BELOW IS VERIFIED against the files in eviVLP/GUI: the residues
# listed are the ones actually labelled NAA in that coat protein file, and
# `kind` was checked against the wild-type residue at that number in
# tmv_resall.pdb / qb_cp1.pdb. Candidate capsids with unverified residue
# numbers are left commented out - a registry with a wrong number is worse
# than one with a gap.
#
# Site literature: Wijesundara et al., "Rip it, stitch it, click it: A
# Chemist's guide to VLP manipulation", Virology 577 (2022) 105-123, Tables 1-7.
# Subunit counts are from that review's text, not derived from our own scripts.

CAPSID  Qbeta   180   Qbeta_capsid_maker.py   fix_a,fix_b,fix_c
CAPSID  TMV     2130  TMV_capsid_maker.py     TMV_naa

# --- TMV ---------------------------------------------------------------------
SITE  TMV    lys  native_K53_K68   tmv_K53_K68.pdb  53,68  native  Finbloom2016_doxorubicin
SITE  TMV    tyr  native_Y139      tmv_Y139.pdb     139    native  Bruckman2013_MRI,Lee2020_superoxide
SITE  TMV    lys  T158K            tmv_K158.pdb     158    mutant  Lumata2024_kTMV_TEMPO

# TMV + cysteine: no file, and deliberately so. TMV has exactly one native
# cysteine, Cys27, and the literature never conjugates it - researchers
# engineer S123C or T103C instead, which is strong evidence it is buried.
# Adding a native-cysteine TMV site would need a solvent-accessibility
# calculation first, not a guess.
#
# NUMBERING NOTE: the review's Table 6 lists TMV tyrosine as "Y129", but
# residue 129 in tmv_resall.pdb is ILE and residue 139 is TYR. The review also
# writes "T153K" where our construct is T158K (153 and 158 are both THR, so
# both are possible sites). K53/K68 and every Qbeta number match the review
# exactly, so this is not a numbering-convention offset - Y129 appears to be a
# typo for Y139. Our files are correct; do not "fix" them to match the paper.

# --- Qbeta -------------------------------------------------------------------
SITE  Qbeta  lys  native_K2_K13_K16  qb_cp1_lys.pdb,qb_cp2_lys.pdb,qb_cp3_lys.pdb  2,13,16  native  Hong2009_BSA_fluorescein,Prasuhn2007_Gd
SITE  Qbeta  cys  native_C74_C80     qb_cp1_cys.pdb,qb_cp2_cys.pdb,qb_cp3_cys.pdb  74,80    native  Leung2017_polymers,Maurer2005_nicotine

# Qbeta C74/C80 form inter-subunit disulfides. Conjugating them requires
# reduction first and may destabilise the capsid - worth telling the user.
#
# Qbeta + tyrosine: NO entry. Qbeta has native Tyr62, Tyr89, Tyr99 and Tyr132,
# but the review records no tyrosine conjugation for Qbeta at all. There is
# also no qb_cp*_tyr.pdb, so ProcessPgnFile currently goes looking for a file
# that does not exist and fails on a missing file rather than saying there is
# no site. A registry lookup would report "no site recorded" instead.

# --- Candidates for expansion (NOT verified - residue numbers missing) -------
# MS2 is the best next capsid: 180 CP like Qbeta, so it reuses the Qbeta
# assembly path, and it has a NUMBERED native site.
#   CAPSID  MS2   180   Qbeta_capsid_maker.py  ...
#   SITE  MS2  tyr  native_Y85  <coat protein>  85  native  Hooker2004_diazonium
#
# CCMV is also 180 CP, but the review gives its sites only as "Lysine" and
# "Cysteine" with no residue numbers, so they must be sourced elsewhere first.
#   CAPSID  CCMV  180   Qbeta_capsid_maker.py  ...
#
# CPMV (60 CP) and PVX (rod) likewise list unnumbered "Lysine"/"Cysteine".
# P22 (420 CP) has NO native sites in the review - every P22 site listed
# (S39C, K110C, K118C, V119C, S133C) is engineered, so P22 is a
# mutant-sites-only capsid.
#
# Not covered by the current three amino acids: TMV's most-used sites in the
# review are E97 and E106, glutamate carboxylates. Supporting carboxylate
# conjugation would be a fourth amino acid, not another coat protein.
